  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{10}2\\sqrt[5]{x}\\,\\text{d}x=A,$ what is the value of ${\\int }_{-10}^{0}2\\sqrt[5]{x}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=2\\sqrt[5]{x}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =2\\sqrt[5]{(-x)} \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt[5]{x} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=2\\sqrt[5]{x}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-10}^{10}2\\sqrt[5]{x}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-10}^{10}2\\sqrt[5]{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-10}^{0}2\\sqrt[5]{x}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{10}2\\sqrt[5]{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-10}^{0}2\\sqrt[5]{x}\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-10}^{0}2\\sqrt[5]{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
